<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Sakarya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Battle of the Sakarya · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War (1919–1922)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Sakarya was fought from 23 August to 13 September 1921 along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakarya-river">Sakarya River</a> west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War</a>. The Turkish nationalist army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> halted the Greek advance on Ankara after three weeks of fighting, forcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> to withdraw. It was the turning point of the war, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-national-assembly-of-turkey">Grand National Assembly</a> awarded Mustafa Kemal the title Gazi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sakarya.jpg" alt="Battle of Sakarya" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sfisek · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Sakarya_1921.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In July 1921 a Greek feint towards Eskişehir drew off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu">İsmet Pasha</a> while the main blow fell at Kara Hisar, and Eskişehir fell on 17 July. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>'s advice İsmet broke off with heavy losses and fell back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakarya-river">Sakarya River</a>, about 80 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>. The river's great loop, with steep banks and few bridges and barren ridges rising beyond it, made strong defensive ground. The Greek command chose to advance and try to destroy the nationalist army, while the Turkish leadership prepared to move the government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> if the line broke.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> committed his army to the assault on 10 August. After nine days of hard marching through country short of food and water, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> made contact south of the Gök River on 23 August and attacked along the whole line on 26 August, storming one entrenched ridge after another. By 2 September they held the heights of Mount Çal, and in the centre they broke through the Turks' second line, some units coming within 50 km of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>. Turkish cavalry raids cut their supplies, while fresh Turkish draftees kept arriving.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> took personal command and on 8 September led a counterattack near Mount Çal that won only limited success. Fearing a larger outflanking move as winter approached, Anastasios Papoulas ordered a general retreat towards Eskişehir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afyonkarahisar">Afyonkarahisar</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> crossed back over the river unmolested, blowing up railways and bridges and burning villages as they went.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-national-assembly-of-turkey">Grand National Assembly</a> also made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> a field marshal. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> the battle is called the Officers' Battle for its officer losses of 70 to 80 per cent. Freed by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> with the Russians and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> with the French, the nationalists turned their strength west and opened their offensive at Dumlupınar on 26 August 1922. Historians still debate the Greek failure: Roderick Beaton holds the battle was very nearly won by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and George Sayen blames an overcautious plan and reserves left unused.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Sakarya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its army stopped the Greeks at <b>Sakarya</b> in 1921 and drove them out in 1922 under Mustafa Kemal and Ismet.</blockquote><a href="/state/government-of-the-grand-national-assembly">Government of the Grand National Assembly</a></li></ul></section>
